      ]
    },
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p29-q9a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Using the same method, find the infinite sum of reciprocals of powers of 5:\n$$1+\\frac15+\\frac1{5^2}+\\frac1{5^3}+\\cdots$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\frac54$",
          "worked": "Let $T$ be the required sum.\n$$\\begin{align*}T&=1+\\frac15\\left(1+\\frac15+\\frac1{5^2}+\\cdots\\right)\\\\T&=1+\\frac15T\\\\T-\\frac15T&=1\\\\\\frac45T&=1\\\\T&=\\frac54.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 56
      },
      {
        "id": "p29-q9b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Hence, find the sum of all unit fractions that are terminating decimals:\n$$1+\\frac12+\\frac14+\\frac15+\\frac18+\\frac1{10}+\\cdots$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\frac52$.\n\nGroup the fractions by the exponent of 5 in their denominators, then use part (a).",
          "worked": "A unit fraction terminates exactly when its denominator is $2^a5^b$, where $a,b\\geq0$ are integers. Group the fractions by $b$. For each fixed $b$, the sum is\n$$\\begin{align*}U_b&=\\frac1{5^b}\\left(1+\\frac12+\\frac1{2^2}+\\cdots\\right)\\\\U_b&=\\frac2{5^b}.\\end{align*}$$\nEvery qualifying denominator occurs once because prime factorisation is unique. Therefore, using part (a), the total is\n$$\\begin{align*}U&=2+\\frac25+\\frac2{5^2}+\\cdots\\\\U&=2\\left(1+\\frac15+\\frac1{5^2}+\\cdots\\right)\\\\U&=2\\times\\frac54\\\\U&=\\frac52.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 90
      }
    ]
  },
